You are absolutely right. Having an inline "Read More" link attached to the end of a cut-off string often looks messy, especially if the text ends on a weird character or a line break.
To make it look like a professional, enterprise-grade application, we should do two things:
 1. Use MUI's **Collapse** component so the description smoothly slides open with an animation.
 2. Move the "Read More / Read Less" trigger to a clean, dedicated button below the text, complete with directional arrow icons.
Here is exactly what to replace.
### The Replacement
Find the SelectedVoucherCard component in your file, and replace the entire <Grid item size="{12}"> block at the bottom with this new, professional layout:
**FIND THIS:**
```javascript
          <Grid item size={12}>
            <Typography variant="caption" color="text.secondary" display="block" gutterBottom>Description</Typography>
            <Typography variant="body2" sx={{ whiteSpace: "pre-wrap", lineHeight: 1.6, fontWeight: 300 }}>
              {isLongDesc && !isDescExpanded ? `${shortenString(desc)}...` : desc}
              {isLongDesc && (
                <Typography 
                  component="span" 
                  color="primary" 
                  onClick={() => setIsDescExpanded(!isDescExpanded)}
                  sx={{ cursor: "pointer", ml: 1, fontWeight: "bold", '&:hover': { textDecoration: 'underline' } }}
                >
                  {isDescExpanded ? "Read Less" : "Read More"}
                </Typography>
              )}
            </Typography>
          </Grid>

```
**REPLACE WITH THIS:**
```javascript
          <Grid item size={12}>
            <Typography variant="caption" color="text.secondary" display="block" gutterBottom>
              Description
            </Typography>
            
            <Box sx={{ position: "relative" }}>
              {/* collapsedSize={44} perfectly frames about 2 lines of body2 text */}
              <Collapse in={!isLongDesc || isDescExpanded} collapsedSize={44}>
                <Typography variant="body2" sx={{ whiteSpace: "pre-wrap", lineHeight: 1.6, fontWeight: 400, color: "text.primary" }}>
                  {desc}
                </Typography>
              </Collapse>
              
              {isLongDesc && (
                <Box sx={{ display: "flex", justifyContent: "flex-start", mt: 0.5 }}>
                  <Button
                    size="small"
                    disableRipple
                    onClick={() => setIsDescExpanded(!isDescExpanded)}
                    endIcon={isDescExpanded ? <KeyboardArrowUpIcon /> : <KeyboardArrowDownIcon />}
                    sx={{
                      p: 0,
                      minWidth: "auto",
                      textTransform: "none",
                      fontWeight: 600,
                      color: "primary.main",
                      "&:hover": { background: "transparent", textDecoration: "underline" }
                    }}
                  >
                    {isDescExpanded ? "Read Less" : "Read More"}
                  </Button>
                </Box>
              )}
            </Box>
          </Grid>

```
### Why this design is better:
 * **Smooth Animation:** Instead of the text violently snapping into place, the Collapse wrapper gracefully slides the box open and closed.
 * **No Awkward Cuts:** Because Collapse handles the height visually, you no longer rely on chopping the string apart with JavaScript. (In fact, you can completely delete your shortenString function from the top of the file now!).
 * **Clear Action Area:** The text is strictly for reading, and the actionable button sits directly below it with a polished icon, aligning perfectly with modern UX standards.
